// Ain -- analogue input reporting, after CiA DS-401.
//
// Decides WHEN a measured value is worth sending. It knows nothing about the
// transport: wire `Out` into a CAN field, an SMS body or a println, and use
// `changed(Out)` as the trigger. `Out` IS the last reported value, so the
// change of it and the value to send are the same fact -- no separate pulse
// to keep in phase.
//
// Two independent reasons to report:
//
//   Delta / DeltaDown -- the value moved far enough to be worth an update.
//     The reference is the last value we REPORTED, not the last sample. That
//     is the whole point: the receiver's copy is then never more than Delta
//     wrong, however slowly the input drifts. Comparing against the last
//     sample instead gives a rate trigger, and a slow ramp never fires.
//     Separate up and down deltas because urgency is often asymmetric --
//     report a falling temperature finely and a rising one coarsely.
//
//   Upper / Lower -- the value crossed out of its normal band. This is an
//     event, not a measurement, so it fires once on the way out and not again
//     until the value has come back inside.
//
// Combined with an inhibit time on the transport you get the full DS-401
// contract per signal: accuracy (Delta), liveness (a cyclic send by the
// caller) and a bound on bus load (inhibit).
//
//   #Ain temp  In <- Temp  Delta=5 DeltaDown=1  Lower=200 Upper=800
//   F.tx = 1 ? changed(temp.Out)
//
#module Ain
#variable In        in  = 0     // raw value -- wire it with `In <- Sensor`
#variable Out       out = 0     // last REPORTED value; changed(Out) = send it
#variable Delta         = 0     // report on a rise of this much since Out (0 = off)
#variable DeltaDown     = 0     // report on a fall of this much since Out (0 = off)
#variable Upper         = 0     // report when In leaves the band upwards
#variable Lower         = 0     // ... or downwards (Upper == Lower = limits off)
#variable Hyst          = 0     // how far back inside the band before it can fire again
#variable Enable:1 unsigned = 1 // the DS-401 global interrupt enable
#variable Above:1 unsigned  = 0 // latch: In is currently out of band, high side
#variable Below:1 unsigned  = 0 // latch: ... low side

// Deadband. Note this also gives the FIRST report for free: Out starts at 0,
// so the opening sample is a large enough move to be sent.
Out = In ? Enable && Delta > 0     && In - Out >= Delta
Out = In ? Enable && DeltaDown > 0 && Out - In >= DeltaDown

// Limit crossings. The latches make it an edge, so sitting outside the band
// reports once rather than every cycle.
Out = In ? Enable && Upper != Lower && In > Upper && !Above
Out = In ? Enable && Upper != Lower && In < Lower && !Below

// The latches release only Hyst back INSIDE the limit. That margin is what
// keeps a value resting on the threshold from chattering. Hyst is its own
// parameter and not Delta: a pure alarm wants hysteresis with the deadband
// switched off, and a pure telemetry channel wants the opposite.
Above = 1 ? Upper != Lower && In > Upper
Above = 0 ? Upper != Lower && In < Upper - Hyst
Below = 1 ? Upper != Lower && In < Lower
Below = 0 ? Upper != Lower && In > Lower + Hyst
#end
